- **`ui` is the view, and a view is a template the server renders (`ui = "rendered"` in `.config/rotproof.toml`).** A
  view is a file of {extensions} in a level of `ui`, which follows atomic design (`pages` > `templates` > `organisms`
  > `molecules` > `atoms`; a level may refer to the levels below it and to its own), and `ui` holds only its levels. A
  template anywhere else in the project fails `rotproof check` as code outside the layers: move it into a level, or,
  when it is no template (a generated page, a report), list its path in `unchecked`. `ui` knows neither `application`,
  `domain` nor `infrastructure`. A project with no screens declares `ui = "none"` instead, and has neither `ui` nor
  `presentation`.
- **A route function in `infrastructure.inbound` renders a template, and `composition` registers the route.** It names
  the template with a string (`render_template("pages/home.html", ...)`), which is no import, so it imports only the
  input ports of `protocol.inbound`, as every entry point does, and passes their data to the template. `presentation`,
  the state of the screens apart from the view, is absent from a new project: a template draws what the route hands it
  and holds no state of its own. Take it out of `absent` and run `rotproof create --yes` to keep a screen's state in a
  model; a template cannot import it, so only a `.py` file of `ui` can.
- **A template refers to another by a path from the directory `ui/` of its unit, which the template loader of the
  project is set to** (Flask's `template_folder`, the `directory` of `Jinja2Templates`, Django's `DIRS`), as in
  `{% extends "templates/base.html" %}` and `{% include "atoms/button.html" %}`. `rotproof check` judges the tags
  `extends`, `include`, `import` and `from` by the level of each file, as it judges an import: `atoms` refer to no
  other level, `organisms` to the levels below them, and no template to a level above it. A reference that is not a
  literal, that climbs out of the directory, or that names no file of the project (a template of a package) is not
  seen. The tags are read as {read_as}, and `rotproof check` says so on every run. When a unit's `pyproject.toml` asks
  for another version of the package of one of them, the references are not read as that language, and when it asks
  for another version of every one they are not judged at all: the check says which and why. Changing what
  `pyproject.toml` asks for to stop the check judging is no fix.
